> ants. I'm not sure where the cutoff point is...

	There's no cutoff.

	I once spent a long time cranking through how effective 
negative ACs were.  Rules of thumb:  if a monster's attack averages
N points of damage, then an AC of -N cuts the damage done in
half, and an AC of -2N cuts it to a quarter.

	Soldier ants have two attacks:  2d4 3d4P, which average 5
and 7.5.  Split the difference, and an AC of -6 will take off about
half the damage from successful attacks.

	At AC -4, you would have been taking about 7 points damage 
per ant per turn (14 per turn when they got double moves on you).

	Note that the high-speed monsters with multiple attacks
tend to have attacks doing little damage.  An AC of even -3 helps
a lot against giant ants and bees.

